1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,280
There are probably a few thousand

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:03,720
photos on your phone.

3
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:05,100
Most of them are screenshots

4
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:06,400
you forgot you took.

5
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:08,160
But your phone has never

6
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:10,060
stored a single picture.

7
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:11,060
Not one.

8
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:13,100
It only stores numbers.

9
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:14,960
Long lists of them.

10
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:15,900
Like this.

11
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:17,260
That's a picture.

12
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:18,060
I promise.

13
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,080
And in a couple of minutes, you're

14
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,400
going to read it yourself.

15
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:25,210
This is stop number five on Bob's trip.

16
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,310
There are four flags behind him already.

17
00:00:27,330 --> 00:00:29,430
At the first one, he found out

18
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:31,510
what a computer really is.

19
00:00:31,610 --> 00:00:33,070
At the second, why it only

20
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:35,130
understands ones and zeros.

21
00:00:35,490 --> 00:00:37,490
Then he learned to count with them.

22
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:39,330
And last time, he stood at the

23
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:41,150
edge of this canyon and sent his

24
00:00:41,170 --> 00:00:43,070
friend a message, one letter at

25
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:45,650
a time, with a row of eight lamps.

26
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:48,630
Today, his friend wants something harder.

27
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:51,270
He wants to see what Bob looks like.

28
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:52,290
Fair enough.

29
00:00:52,630 --> 00:00:53,910
From over there, Bob is

30
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:55,530
about the size of a bean.

31
00:00:55,550 --> 00:00:58,290
But the lamps can only send numbers.

32
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:01,670
So how do you turn a face into numbers?

33
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:03,530
Start with the screen you're

34
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:04,910
watching right now.

35
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:07,190
Get close to it, way closer than

36
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:09,050
your eyes can handle, and the picture

37
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:11,570
breaks apart into tiny squares.

38
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:13,950
Each square is called a pixel.

39
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:16,770
And a pixel can only do one job.

40
00:01:16,930 --> 00:01:18,410
It shows one color.

41
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:20,930
That's all a picture on a screen is.

42
00:01:21,030 --> 00:01:23,570
A grid of colored squares, packed so

43
00:01:23,590 --> 00:01:25,990
tight that your eyes blend them together.

44
00:01:26,586 --> 00:01:28,986
So let's start as easy as it gets.

45
00:01:29,146 --> 00:01:31,806
Just two colors, light and dark.

46
00:01:32,046 --> 00:01:33,826
A light square is a one.

47
00:01:33,946 --> 00:01:35,766
A dark square is a zero.

48
00:01:36,346 --> 00:01:38,846
Bob already has eight lamps, so he can

49
00:01:38,866 --> 00:01:41,586
send one row of eight squares at a time.

50
00:01:41,786 --> 00:01:43,446
He flashes the first row, and

51
00:01:43,466 --> 00:01:45,166
his friend paints it on the wall.

52
00:01:45,466 --> 00:01:46,486
Then the next row.

53
00:01:46,726 --> 00:01:47,506
Then the next.

54
00:01:47,866 --> 00:01:50,446
If you want to beat him to it, pause now.

55
00:01:52,186 --> 00:01:53,286
Those are the same numbers

56
00:01:53,306 --> 00:01:54,806
from the start of the video.

57
00:01:54,826 --> 00:01:56,106
You just read a picture.

58
00:01:56,266 --> 00:01:57,026
It's Bob!

59
00:01:57,426 --> 00:01:58,286
Sort of.

60
00:01:58,426 --> 00:02:00,126
You can make out the hat, and

61
00:02:00,146 --> 00:02:01,686
there's a smile under it.

62
00:02:02,206 --> 00:02:03,406
There's one catch.

63
00:02:03,646 --> 00:02:04,866
His friend has to know

64
00:02:04,886 --> 00:02:06,466
where each row ends.

65
00:02:06,686 --> 00:02:07,966
If he thinks the rows are only

66
00:02:07,986 --> 00:02:10,186
four squares wide, the exact same

67
00:02:10,206 --> 00:02:11,786
numbers come out like this.

68
00:02:12,166 --> 00:02:14,426
So before any picture, a computer

69
00:02:14,446 --> 00:02:16,306
sends two numbers first.

70
00:02:16,506 --> 00:02:18,566
How wide it is, and how tall.

71
00:02:19,256 --> 00:02:22,096
Still, two colors isn't much to work with.

72
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:23,816
So instead of one lamp per

73
00:02:23,836 --> 00:02:26,316
square, Bob uses all eight.

74
00:02:26,456 --> 00:02:28,836
And eight lamps can count from zero all

75
00:02:28,856 --> 00:02:31,616
the way up to two hundred and fifty-five.

76
00:02:31,636 --> 00:02:32,913
Zero means black.

77
00:02:32,933 --> 00:02:35,416
Two hundred and fifty-five means white.

78
00:02:35,696 --> 00:02:36,736
Every number in between

79
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:38,276
is a shade of gray.

80
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:40,340
Color surprised me the first

81
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:41,920
time I learned how it works.

82
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:44,240
Every pixel on your screen is really

83
00:02:44,260 --> 00:02:47,420
three tiny lights, sitting side by side.

84
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:50,240
A red one, a green one, and a blue one.

85
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:52,100
There's no yellow light in there.

86
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:54,660
When red and green turn on together, your

87
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:56,900
eyes just decide they're seeing yellow.

88
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,260
Turn all three up and you get white.

89
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:01,620
Turn them all off and you get black.

90
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:04,900
So any color is just three numbers.

91
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:06,880
How much red, how much green,

92
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:07,700
and how much blue.

93
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:10,930
Bob's shirt, for example, is forty red,

94
00:03:10,950 --> 00:03:13,340
a hundred green, and two hundred blue.

95
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:16,250
Now Bob can send the real thing.

96
00:03:16,470 --> 00:03:19,010
A face sixteen squares wide and sixteen

97
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:21,910
tall, with three numbers for every square.

98
00:03:22,150 --> 00:03:22,883
That's seven hundred and

99
00:03:22,903 --> 00:03:24,590
sixty-eight numbers.

100
00:03:24,990 --> 00:03:26,130
It takes a while.

101
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:32,220
And there he is.

102
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:34,120
Lucky for Bob, his face was

103
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:35,660
already made of squares.

104
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:39,180
Your phone does exactly the same thing.

105
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,200
It just uses a lot more squares.

106
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:42,900
A normal photo has about

107
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:44,580
twelve million pixels.

108
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:45,980
Three numbers each.

109
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:48,260
That's thirty-six million numbers,

110
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:50,540
for one picture of your lunch.

111
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:52,480
And your screen reads them and sets

112
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,160
every one of those tiny lights

113
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:56,880
again, about sixty times a second.

114
00:03:57,630 --> 00:03:59,730
So that screenshot you forgot about?

115
00:03:59,770 --> 00:04:01,410
It's a list of numbers, with a

116
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:02,790
width and a height at the front.

117
00:04:02,810 --> 00:04:05,690
Your phone reads the list and turns on millions

118
00:04:05,710 --> 00:04:08,670
of tiny red, green and blue lights.

119
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:09,790
That's a picture.

120
00:04:10,290 --> 00:04:12,710
Bob plants flag number five.

121
00:04:12,730 --> 00:04:15,190
The desert's almost behind him now.

122
00:04:15,210 --> 00:04:17,150
And up ahead, somewhere in that

123
00:04:17,170 --> 00:04:19,450
fog, something is smoking.
